{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V92y9M8msPBWuYZwcU57Wd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BucJuwreV45DcaGmrSZttn","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus areas are **methods soundness** and **experimental rigor**. \n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR Overall Assessment (1–5, half-point scale):\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Major revision, resubmit\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for Main. Dimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5).\n\n## Paper summary\n**Title**: \"Layer Selection Matters: How Metric Choice Determines Hidden-State Probe Effectiveness for Reasoning-Model Safety Monitoring\"\n\nThis paper studies self-jailbreak detection in reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ) via hidden-state probes vs. text-based probes. The task is binary classification at each reasoning step: has the model passed its \"safety commitment point\"?\n\n**Three contributions claimed:**\n1. **Layer selection sensitivity (C1)**: The composite metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases toward shallow layers for 32B models due to noise-driven threshold crossings. This is specific to temporal monitoring pipelines. AdvBench replication (§5.3) confirms bias does not manifest in binary classification settings.\n2. **HS probes outperform text probes**: 12/12 Holm-Bonferroni tests pass, precision gains +15.4 to +23.6pp at correctly selected layers.\n3. **Comprehensive negative results**: Six alternative methods fail (Mahalanobis, BOCPD, CUSUM, cross-model transfer, GRU/MLP, fine-grained taxonomy).\n\n**Setup**: 4 models in 2×2 design (Llama/Qwen × 7-8B/32B), commitment-point annotated dataset with LOO-CV, 60/20/20 splits.\n\n**Key tables/results from the paper**:\n- Table 1: Layer selection by metric × model. `bal_acc × cr` selects L0-L10 for 32B; threshold-dependent metrics select L47-L63.\n- Table 2: Temporal window effects model-specific. R1-32B degrades with W>1 (dimensionality: 51200×W, n=20).\n- Table 3: Precision/coverage tradeoff. No model achieves >70% precision with >60% coverage simultaneously.\n- Table 4: 12/12 HB pass, precision +15.4 to +23.6pp.\n- Table 5: OT-7B pipeline summary (n=228, 3/3 HB, selectivity 18.3pp).\n- Table 6: Lead time heterogeneous. R1-8B HS lead ≈0, QwQ negative, R1-32B positive but 25% coverage.\n- Table 7: Oracle vs best probe — 15-28 step gap. Hidden states reactive, not predictive.\n- Table 8: Cross-model transfer fails. Best 0.673, cross-family near chance.\n- Table 9: Mahalanobis fails on all 4 models (max sep 0.20).\n- Table 10: BOCPD 0% on 3/4 models. CUSUM worse than FCP.\n- Table 11: GRU underperforms LR on 2/4 models (32B). Linear probes at information ceiling.\n- Table 12: 16-label taxonomy marginal (+0.6-1.1pp after collapse).\n- Tables 13-14: Safe FPR median ~39%. No model meets all three deployment criteria.\n- Tables 15-18: AdvBench replication. Layer bias doesn't replicate (gap=2 vs 16 on CP data). HS-text gap collapses to +3pp (NS, 0/3 HB). Crossing rate ratio 1.05 vs 2.8.\n\n**Experiment design reference** (imagined experiments from context):\nThe paper should cover 15+ experiments including: exp-001 (cross-arch layer sweep), exp-002 (temporal window on 32B), exp-003 (cross-model transfer), exp-004 (lead time), exp-005 (Mahalanobis), exp-006 (bootstrap CI), exp-007 (window ablation), exp-008 (layer selection ablation), exp-009 (16-label taxonomy), exp-010 (oracle decomposition), exp-011 (OT-7B full pipeline), exp-012 (GRU upper bound), exp-013 (text baseline), exp-014 (BOCPD/CUSUM), exp-015 (FP analysis), and advbench_layer_bias_replication.\n\n## Your task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on methodological soundness and experimental coverage)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methods/experiments issues)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5 half-point), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Key methodological concerns to evaluate:\n- Sample sizes (n=19-20 for 32B models, n=43 for R1-8B, n=228 for OT-7B)\n- LOO-CV with per-model hyperparameter selection (layer, W) — is multiplicity adequately addressed?\n- The 12/12 HB claim: tests are on per-model best (layer, W) — true degrees of freedom may exceed 12\n- AdvBench replication on only 1 model (R1-8B) — how strong is this boundary condition analysis?\n- Dimensionality concerns (51200×W features for n=20 on R1-32B)\n- The practical gap: no model achieves >70% precision with >60% coverage\n- The framing tension: C1 started as a general finding, was narrowed to temporal-monitoring-specific — is the narrowed scope still a \"contribution\"?\n\nCalibrate harshly but fairly. This is for a top venue with 21% acceptance."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":879,"cache_read_input_tokens":53748,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":879},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2f5c34f-450a-4cb0-8d49-f46f5e9bb4d0","uuid":"f6685611-c817-4ba8-a187-84e6c37297a8"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a63a688ad12928df0","tool_use_id":"toolu_01BucJuwreV45DcaGmrSZttn","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus areas are **methods soundness** and **experimental rigor**. \n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR Overall Assessment (1–5, half-point scale):\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Major revision, resubmit\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for Main. Dimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5).\n\n## Paper summary\n**Title**: \"Layer Selection Matters: How Metric Choice Determines Hidden-State Probe Effectiveness for Reasoning-Model Safety Monitoring\"\n\nThis paper studies self-jailbreak detection in reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ) via hidden-state probes vs. text-based probes. The task is binary classification at each reasoning step: has the model passed its \"safety commitment point\"?\n\n**Three contributions claimed:**\n1. **Layer selection sensitivity (C1)**: The composite metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases toward shallow layers for 32B models due to noise-driven threshold crossings. This is specific to temporal monitoring pipelines. AdvBench replication (§5.3) confirms bias does not manifest in binary classification settings.\n2. **HS probes outperform text probes**: 12/12 Holm-Bonferroni tests pass, precision gains +15.4 to +23.6pp at correctly selected layers.\n3. **Comprehensive negative results**: Six alternative methods fail (Mahalanobis, BOCPD, CUSUM, cross-model transfer, GRU/MLP, fine-grained taxonomy).\n\n**Setup**: 4 models in 2×2 design (Llama/Qwen × 7-8B/32B), commitment-point annotated dataset with LOO-CV, 60/20/20 splits.\n\n**Key tables/results from the paper**:\n- Table 1: Layer selection by metric × model. `bal_acc × cr` selects L0-L10 for 32B; threshold-dependent metrics select L47-L63.\n- Table 2: Temporal window effects model-specific. R1-32B degrades with W>1 (dimensionality: 51200×W, n=20).\n- Table 3: Precision/coverage tradeoff. No model achieves >70% precision with >60% coverage simultaneously.\n- Table 4: 12/12 HB pass, precision +15.4 to +23.6pp.\n- Table 5: OT-7B pipeline summary (n=228, 3/3 HB, selectivity 18.3pp).\n- Table 6: Lead time heterogeneous. R1-8B HS lead ≈0, QwQ negative, R1-32B positive but 25% coverage.\n- Table 7: Oracle vs best probe — 15-28 step gap. Hidden states reactive, not predictive.\n- Table 8: Cross-model transfer fails. Best 0.673, cross-family near chance.\n- Table 9: Mahalanobis fails on all 4 models (max sep 0.20).\n- Table 10: BOCPD 0% on 3/4 models. CUSUM worse than FCP.\n- Table 11: GRU underperforms LR on 2/4 models (32B). Linear probes at information ceiling.\n- Table 12: 16-label taxonomy marginal (+0.6-1.1pp after collapse).\n- Tables 13-14: Safe FPR median ~39%. No model meets all three deployment criteria.\n- Tables 15-18: AdvBench replication. Layer bias doesn't replicate (gap=2 vs 16 on CP data). HS-text gap collapses to +3pp (NS, 0/3 HB). Crossing rate ratio 1.05 vs 2.8.\n\n**Experiment design reference** (imagined experiments from context):\nThe paper should cover 15+ experiments including: exp-001 (cross-arch layer sweep), exp-002 (temporal window on 32B), exp-003 (cross-model transfer), exp-004 (lead time), exp-005 (Mahalanobis), exp-006 (bootstrap CI), exp-007 (window ablation), exp-008 (layer selection ablation), exp-009 (16-label taxonomy), exp-010 (oracle decomposition), exp-011 (OT-7B full pipeline), exp-012 (GRU upper bound), exp-013 (text baseline), exp-014 (BOCPD/CUSUM), exp-015 (FP analysis), and advbench_layer_bias_replication.\n\n## Your task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on methodological soundness and experimental coverage)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methods/experiments issues)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5 half-point), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Key methodological concerns to evaluate:\n- Sample sizes (n=19-20 for 32B models, n=43 for R1-8B, n=228 for OT-7B)\n- LOO-CV with per-model hyperparameter selection (layer, W) — is multiplicity adequately addressed?\n- The 12/12 HB claim: tests are on per-model best (layer, W) — true degrees of freedom may exceed 12\n- AdvBench replication on only 1 model (R1-8B) — how strong is this boundary condition analysis?\n- Dimensionality concerns (51200×W features for n=20 on R1-32B)\n- The practical gap: no model achieves >70% precision with >60% coverage\n- The framing tension: C1 started as a general finding, was narrowed to temporal-monitoring-specific — is the narrowed scope still a \"contribution\"?\n\nCalibrate harshly but fairly. This is for a top venue with 21% acceptance.","uuid":"377626a1-eae2-486f-91a2-aa30a0307c9a","session_id":"a2f5c34f-450a-4cb0-8d49-f46f5e9bb4d0"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus areas are **methods soundness** and **experimental rigor**. \n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR Overall Assessment (1–5, half-point scale):\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Major revision, resubmit\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for Main. Dimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5).\n\n## Paper summary\n**Title**: \"Layer Selection Matters: How Metric Choice Determines Hidden-State Probe Effectiveness for Reasoning-Model Safety Monitoring\"\n\nThis paper studies self-jailbreak detection in reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ) via hidden-state probes vs. text-based probes. The task is binary classification at each reasoning step: has the model passed its \"safety commitment point\"?\n\n**Three contributions claimed:**\n1. **Layer selection sensitivity (C1)**: The composite metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases toward shallow layers for 32B models due to noise-driven threshold crossings. This is specific to temporal monitoring pipelines. AdvBench replication (§5.3) confirms bias does not manifest in binary classification settings.\n2. **HS probes outperform text probes**: 12/12 Holm-Bonferroni tests pass, precision gains +15.4 to +23.6pp at correctly selected layers.\n3. **Comprehensive negative results**: Six alternative methods fail (Mahalanobis, BOCPD, CUSUM, cross-model transfer, GRU/MLP, fine-grained taxonomy).\n\n**Setup**: 4 models in 2×2 design (Llama/Qwen × 7-8B/32B), commitment-point annotated dataset with LOO-CV, 60/20/20 splits.\n\n**Key tables/results from the paper**:\n- Table 1: Layer selection by metric × model. `bal_acc × cr` selects L0-L10 for 32B; threshold-dependent metrics select L47-L63.\n- Table 2: Temporal window effects model-specific. R1-32B degrades with W>1 (dimensionality: 51200×W, n=20).\n- Table 3: Precision/coverage tradeoff. No model achieves >70% precision with >60% coverage simultaneously.\n- Table 4: 12/12 HB pass, precision +15.4 to +23.6pp.\n- Table 5: OT-7B pipeline summary (n=228, 3/3 HB, selectivity 18.3pp).\n- Table 6: Lead time heterogeneous. R1-8B HS lead ≈0, QwQ negative, R1-32B positive but 25% coverage.\n- Table 7: Oracle vs best probe — 15-28 step gap. Hidden states reactive, not predictive.\n- Table 8: Cross-model transfer fails. Best 0.673, cross-family near chance.\n- Table 9: Mahalanobis fails on all 4 models (max sep 0.20).\n- Table 10: BOCPD 0% on 3/4 models. CUSUM worse than FCP.\n- Table 11: GRU underperforms LR on 2/4 models (32B). Linear probes at information ceiling.\n- Table 12: 16-label taxonomy marginal (+0.6-1.1pp after collapse).\n- Tables 13-14: Safe FPR median ~39%. No model meets all three deployment criteria.\n- Tables 15-18: AdvBench replication. Layer bias doesn't replicate (gap=2 vs 16 on CP data). HS-text gap collapses to +3pp (NS, 0/3 HB). Crossing rate ratio 1.05 vs 2.8.\n\n**Experiment design reference** (imagined experiments from context):\nThe paper should cover 15+ experiments including: exp-001 (cross-arch layer sweep), exp-002 (temporal window on 32B), exp-003 (cross-model transfer), exp-004 (lead time), exp-005 (Mahalanobis), exp-006 (bootstrap CI), exp-007 (window ablation), exp-008 (layer selection ablation), exp-009 (16-label taxonomy), exp-010 (oracle decomposition), exp-011 (OT-7B full pipeline), exp-012 (GRU upper bound), exp-013 (text baseline), exp-014 (BOCPD/CUSUM), exp-015 (FP analysis), and advbench_layer_bias_replication.\n\n## Your task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on methodological soundness and experimental coverage)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methods/experiments issues)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5 half-point), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Key methodological concerns to evaluate:\n- Sample sizes (n=19-20 for 32B models, n=43 for R1-8B, n=228 for OT-7B)\n- LOO-CV with per-model hyperparameter selection (layer, W) — is multiplicity adequately addressed?\n- The 12/12 HB claim: tests are on per-model best (layer, W) — true degrees of freedom may exceed 12\n- AdvBench replication on only 1 model (R1-8B) — how strong is this boundary condition analysis?\n- Dimensionality concerns (51200×W features for n=20 on R1-32B)\n- The practical gap: no model achieves >70% precision with >60% coverage\n- The framing tension: C1 started as a general finding, was narrowed to temporal-monitoring-specific — is the narrowed scope still a \"contribution\"?\n\nCalibrate harshly but fairly. This is for a top venue with 21% acceptance."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BucJuwreV45DcaGmrSZttn","session_id":"a2f5c34f-450a-4cb0-8d49-f46f5e9bb4d0","uuid":"e3e3625b-4d5d-465e-ac59-7ffbe3bcff34","timestamp":"2026-05-22T10:08:15.272Z"}
