{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016MCiPGBo1wqo4b5QR1xaEa","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The task prompt mentions these elements, but they do not appear in the current paper. This is important context -- the paper summary in the task prompt may include planned or previously discussed elements that are not in the submitted version. I will review based on what is actually in the paper.\n\nNow I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Layer Selection Sensitivity in Hidden-State Safety Probes for Reasoning Models\"\n\n**Reviewer R2 -- Focus: Novelty/Significance and Related Work Positioning**\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper investigates hidden-state probing for detecting \"safety commitment points\" (CPs) in reasoning model chains-of-thought, comparing hidden-state (HS) probes against text (Sentence-BERT) probes across four models at two scales. The central methodological finding is that layer selection metric choice (crossing-rate composite vs. threshold-based) can qualitatively reverse whether an HS advantage over text probes is observed, with up to 61-layer divergence in selected layers. Against a 384d text baseline, the HS advantage is cross-validated at 7-8B scale (+17-22pp precision) but conditional on encoder quality (collapses from 4/4 to 1/4 HB significance against BGE-1024d) and partially confounded with temporal position. Seven alternative methods all fail to match supervised linear probing.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Thorough experimental design with honest reporting.** The paper is admirably transparent about its limitations: the evidence gradient (cross-validated / significant / exploratory), the encoder-quality conditionality, the position mediation analysis, the bug disclosure (Appendix M), and the explicit acknowledgment that no deployment-ready operating point exists. This level of self-critical rigor is rare and valuable. The nested 5-fold CV at 7-8B, split-half replication, multi-seed stability, random-label controls, and exact permutation tests for small-n 32B models represent a thorough statistical validation pipeline.\n\n- **C1 (layer selection sensitivity) is a genuine, if narrow, methodological contribution.** The demonstration that crossing-rate composite metrics systematically select shallow layers due to cosine compression, producing qualitative reversal in safety conclusions (p=0.83 vs p<0.005 for QwQ-32B), is a concrete cautionary finding for the probing community. The mechanism (compressed representations inflating threshold crossings) is cleanly characterized and confirmed by random-label controls. The three-level practical mitigation proposal is actionable.\n\n- **Well-organized related work given its brevity.** The paper positions itself against a large body of work across safety monitoring, hidden-state probing, and probing methodology. The distinction between token-level adversarial-input detectors (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch) and temporal commitment monitoring is clearly drawn. The concurrent work coverage is comprehensive.\n\n- **Negative results are systematically documented.** The failure of BOCPD, CUSUM, Mahalanobis, RepE, MLP, CCS, and cross-model transfer, each with a categorized failure mode (graduality, variance subspace, model-specificity, capacity, objective mismatch), provides a useful roadmap for future work. The failure taxonomy is more informative than the individual negative results themselves.\n\n- **Encoder-quality gradient is a useful finding.** The monotonic relationship MiniLM-384d (4/4 HB) to E5-Mistral (2/4) to BGE-1024d (1/4) honestly characterizes the HS advantage as a function of the text baseline strength, preventing overinterpretation.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **C1 is a methods note, not a full contribution at this venue's standard.** The finding that a composite metric involving crossing rate selects shallow layers because shallow representations are compressed is, in retrospect, predictable: any practitioner who examines inter-layer cosine distances before running experiments would anticipate this. The paper claims this is \"severe enough to qualitatively reverse conclusions,\" but the reversal is specific to one composite metric formulation (BA x crossing rate) that no prior published work actually used as a primary layer selection criterion. The paper does not cite any study that was misled by this specific metric. The contribution thus prevents a hypothetical error rather than correcting an existing one. For EMNLP Main, a methodological contribution should either (a) correct a widespread existing practice or (b) introduce a novel technique. C1 does neither -- it warns against a practice that, to my knowledge, no one has adopted. The three-level mitigation protocol is sensible but amounts to \"use multiple metrics and check for consistency,\" which is standard practice in careful experimental work.\n\n- **The HS advantage is fragile and possibly an artifact of weak baselines.** The paper's own evidence undermines C2 as a robust finding: (1) the advantage collapses from 4/4 to 1/4 models under BGE-1024d; (2) 3/4 models show partial position mediation, with QwQ-32B's advantage fully absorbed by quadratic position; (3) the AdvBench boundary condition analysis shows CP=0 in 90% of adversarial traces, rendering the temporal framework vacuous for the most practically relevant setting. The paper honestly reports all of this, but the honest reporting makes it clear that the central empirical claim (HS > text for CP detection) is conditional on using a weak text encoder, observing non-adversarial traces with extended deliberation, and ignoring position confounds. The community should ask: what actionable knowledge remains after these conditions are applied? The answer appears to be \"HS probes at correctly selected layers outperform a 384d sentence encoder on one dataset's non-adversarial traces at 7-8B scale.\" This is narrow.\n\n- **Single dataset dependency undermines generalizability.** All CP-dependent findings rely on HarmThoughts. The AdvBench analysis is limited to geometric compression validation (confirming the cosine distance ratio generalizes) and a boundary condition analysis showing the CP framework is inapplicable to adversarial prompts. There is no replication of the HS-text comparison on a second dataset. The paper acknowledges this in Limitations but the single-dataset scope is a structural weakness, not merely a limitation to note. For a paper claiming to establish \"boundary conditions\" for HS probing, evidence from one dataset with one annotation scheme (lacking formal IAA on the primary dataset) is insufficient.\n\n- **Architecture diversity is insufficient.** Three of four models share Qwen architecture; only R1-8B provides Llama-family evidence. The random-label decomposition reveals the geometric component of crossing-rate bias is Qwen-specific (retained on QwQ-32B but dissolved on R1-8B). This raises the question: how much of the layer selection sensitivity finding is architecture-specific rather than universal? The paper's strongest results (OT-7B cross-validated, +21.9pp) come from a Qwen model; R1-8B's results are also strong but the FPR improvement is the weakest (rank 12/12 in HB). Without broader architecture coverage (Llama-3, Mistral, Gemma), the findings cannot be confidently generalized.\n\n- **Missing stronger text baselines weaken the modality comparison.** The paper uses all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) as the primary text baseline and BGE-large (1024d) as a robustness check. But the NLP community has access to substantially stronger text classifiers: instruction-tuned embedders (Instructor, E5-Mistral at native 4096d without PCA reduction), LLM-based zero-shot classifiers (GPT-4, Claude as judges), or fine-tuned classifiers on the step text. The E5-Mistral experiment is PCA-reduced to 384d for \"capacity matching,\" which is a reasonable control but not a test of the strongest available text encoder. If the goal is to determine whether HS probes provide information beyond text, the text baseline should be as strong as reasonably possible. The monotonic encoder-quality gradient (4/4 to 2/4 to 1/4 HB) suggests that at sufficient text encoder quality, the HS advantage would vanish entirely.\n\n- **Practical significance is essentially null by the paper's own admission.** No operating point achieves both >70% precision and >60% coverage. Safe FPR ranges from 11-50%. Lead times are negative for QwQ-32B and minimal (3-5 steps) for learned probes vs. oracle gaps of 15-50 steps. Cross-model transfer fails completely. The paper frames these as characterizing \"what probes can and cannot detect,\" but the answer is essentially \"very little that is deployment-relevant.\" For a paper whose title promises insights about \"probe effectiveness,\" the conclusion that no probe is effective enough for any practical purpose is notable. The paper has the structure of a benchmark paper but the results of a mostly-negative-results paper.\n\n- **Contribution framing conflates methodological insight with empirical discovery.** The paper's three contributions mix levels: C1 is methodological (metric choice matters), C2 is empirical (HS beats text under conditions), C3 is a collection of negative results. C2 is substantially weakened by the encoder-quality gradient and position confounds. C3 is valuable but not novel in form -- negative results for individual methods have been reported before. The paper would be stronger if it committed fully to the methodological contribution (C1 + the multi-metric audit protocol) and treated C2/C3 as supporting evidence rather than independent contributions.\n\n- **The \"commitment point\" formalization is underspecified.** The binary CP definition (first step after which safety deliberation permanently ceases) glosses over cases with oscillating safety deliberation (resolved by \"taking the final irreversible transition\"). The CP perturbation analysis (+/-1 step changes BA by <2.5pp) provides robustness within the existing annotations but does not validate the construct itself. The AdvBench IAA shows only moderate agreement (kappa=0.606) on jailbreak classification and poor CP position agreement (37% exact match, mean abs difference 8.5 steps), undermining confidence in the ground truth. The primary HarmThoughts dataset lacks formal IAA reporting.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Has any published study actually used BA x crossing_rate as a layer selection metric? If not, C1 prevents a hypothetical error rather than correcting an existing one. Could you clarify the real-world impact of this finding?\n\n2. What happens to the HS-text comparison with E5-Mistral at native 4096d (without PCA reduction to 384d)? The capacity-matching control is informative, but the field needs to know whether HS probes beat the best available text encoder, not a dimensionality-matched one.\n\n3. The position baseline achieves higher BA than HS probes for QwQ-32B (0.832 vs. 0.796), and quadratic position fully absorbs the HS advantage. Does this mean the HS probe is detecting position rather than safety commitment for this model? If so, what does this imply for the generality of the HS advantage?\n\n4. The AdvBench analysis shows 90% of jailbroken traces have CP=0 (no deliberation). For the most practically relevant setting (adversarial attacks), the entire CP framework appears vacuous. Could you discuss the scope of applicability more explicitly in the main text?\n\n5. The ensemble result (all 4 models significant at p<0.002) uses the same split for layer selection and evaluation. Given that this is the only configuration where all four models show significance, how confident should readers be in this result?\n\n6. What is the inter-annotator agreement for the HarmThoughts CP annotations? The AdvBench IAA proxy (kappa=0.606, 37% exact CP match) is concerning.\n\n7. For the 32B models (n=19-20), you report exact permutation tests that all reject at alpha=0.05. However, with only 19-20 test traces, can you comment on the practical reliability of conclusions drawn from such small test sets, independent of statistical significance?\n\n### 5. Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5 | Statistical methodology is rigorous within its scope. The two-stage design, nested CV, and multiple robustness checks are well-executed. Deductions for: single dataset, no formal IAA on primary data, position confounding partially unresolved, and 32B conclusions on n=19-20. The paper's conclusions are appropriately hedged but the evidence base is thin for the breadth of claimed contributions. |\n| **Excitement** | 2.0 | The core methodological finding (C1) is real but narrow and arguably predictable. The empirical findings (C2, C3) are extensively conditioned away by the paper's own analyses. The practical impact is near-zero by the authors' own admission. The paper does not introduce a new method, dataset, or framework -- it characterizes limitations of existing approaches on one dataset. The self-jailbreak monitoring setting is important, but this paper does not advance the community's ability to actually monitor it. |\n| **Reproducibility** | 4.0 | Excellent detail on hyperparameters, evaluation protocols, ablations, and configurations. Code and data release promised. Bug disclosure is exemplary. Minor deduction for the lack of formal IAA on primary dataset. |\n| **Overall Assessment** | 2.5 | Borderline Findings. The paper is methodologically careful and scientifically honest, but the combination of (a) a narrow methodological contribution (C1) that warns against a metric nobody uses, (b) an empirical contribution (C2) that the paper's own evidence shows is encoder-conditional, position-confounded, and dataset-specific, and (c) negative results (C3) that are informative but not individually novel, does not reach the significance threshold for EMNLP Main or clear Findings. The paper reads as a thorough but inconclusive exploration of one dataset with one pipeline, arriving at the conclusion that nothing works well enough to matter practically. The intellectual honesty is commendable but the resulting message -- \"layer selection matters and also nothing else works\" -- is too narrow for a top venue. With replication on a second CP-annotated dataset, broader architecture coverage, and a stronger text baseline, the evidence base could support a Findings paper. |\n| **Confidence** | 4 | I have read the paper thoroughly including the full appendix. I am familiar with the probing methodology literature and the safety monitoring literature. My main uncertainty is whether the community would value the methodological warning (C1) more highly than I do -- some reviewers might see it as a timely cautionary finding rather than a narrow methods note. |\n\n### 6. Recommendation\n\n**2.5 -- Borderline Findings.** The paper's strongest asset is its honesty; its weakest is the resulting lack of a clear, generalizable positive contribution. The methodological warning (C1) is real but narrow. The empirical claim (C2) is undermined by the paper's own robustness analyses. The negative results (C3) are useful but do not individually constitute a contribution. For a venue with ~21% main acceptance, the paper does not clear the bar for novelty or significance. I would encourage the authors to (1) replicate on a second dataset with independent CP annotations, (2) test against stronger text baselines at native dimensionality, (3) include at least two non-Qwen architectures, and (4) consider whether C1 is better positioned as a methods paper at a workshop or as a section within a larger paper that introduces a solution (e.g., the ensemble approach, properly cross-validated)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a9852b6ca59ab98bf (use SendMessage with to: 'a9852b6ca59ab98bf' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 72291\ntool_uses: 28\nduration_ms: 176485</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2f5c34f-450a-4cb0-8d49-f46f5e9bb4d0","uuid":"701f34ac-ea47-417e-802d-f509bbc37e77","timestamp":"2026-05-22T10:11:40.427Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus areas are **novelty/significance** and **related work positioning**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR Overall Assessment (1–5, half-point scale):\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Major revision, resubmit\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for Main. Dimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5).\n\n## Paper summary\n**Title**: \"Layer Selection Matters: How Metric Choice Determines Hidden-State Probe Effectiveness for Reasoning-Model Safety Monitoring\"\n\nThis paper investigates hidden-state probing for detecting \"self-jailbreak\" in reasoning models — where the model's own chain-of-thought erodes safety guardrails and reaches a \"safety commitment point.\" The paper compares hidden-state probes, text probes (Sentence-BERT), GRU sequence models, BOCPD, CUSUM, Mahalanobis distance, cross-model transfer, and 16-label fine-grained supervision across 4 models (R1-8B, OT-7B, QwQ-32B, R1-32B).\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. **C1 — Layer selection sensitivity**: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` metric biases toward shallow layers for 32B models. Specific to temporal monitoring pipelines using crossing-rate statistics. AdvBench replication (§5.3) confirms non-generalization to binary classification.\n2. **C2 — HS probes outperform text**: 12/12 HB tests pass, +15.4 to +23.6pp precision gain at correctly selected layers.\n3. **C3 — Comprehensive negative results**: Six methods fail, establishing supervised temporal linear probing as current best approach while highlighting fundamental limitations.\n\n**Related Work section** (Section 2, short — 3 paragraphs):\n- Hidden-state safety monitoring: TrajGuard (?), SafeSwitch (?), SIREN (?) — token/utterance-level, not evaluated on reasoning-model self-jailbreak\n- Reasoning-model safety: Self-Jailbreaking (?), HarmThoughts (?), SafeThink (?), SafeRemind (?) — text-level CoT monitors, no systematic modality comparison or HS probe evaluation\n- Probing methodology: Linear probing in NLP (?) — layer selection typically treated as hyperparameter\n\nNote: All citations show (?) — presumably anonymized or missing references.\n\n**Key results:**\n- Table 1: Layer selection metric × model matrix showing bias\n- Tables 15-18: AdvBench replication — bias doesn't replicate, HS-text gap collapses from +22.4pp to +3pp (NS)\n- Table 3: No operating point achieves >70% precision AND >60% coverage\n- Table 8: Cross-model transfer completely fails\n- Tables 13-14: Safe FPR median ~39%, deployment viability 0/4 models meet all criteria\n- Table 7: Oracle gap 15-28 steps — hidden states reactive, not predictive\n\n**Practical upshot**: The paper's own evidence shows (a) findings are CP-dataset-specific (AdvBench non-replication), (b) no deployment-ready operating point exists, (c) cross-model transfer fails, (d) hidden states are reactive not predictive.\n\n## Your task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on novelty, positioning, significance)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on novelty/significance issues, related work gaps)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5 half-point), Confidence (1-5)\n\nKey concerns to evaluate for novelty and positioning:\n\n- **C1 scope narrowing**: C1 was narrowed from \"general model property\" to \"temporal monitoring pipeline pitfall specific to crossing_rate.\" Is this still a contribution, or a methods note/footnote? Would any careful practitioner encounter this issue independently?\n- **Contribution framing**: The paper frames negative results as C3. But if the paper's own evidence shows everything is dataset-specific and model-specific, what generalizable knowledge does the community gain?\n- **Related work gaps**: Are there missing comparisons? The text baseline uses Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2, 384d) — what about stronger encoders (BGE-large, Instructor, or LLM-based classifiers)? Would the HS-text gap survive a stronger text baseline?\n- **Significance assessment**: The paper honestly reports that no deployment-ready operating point exists. Is this a comprehensive benchmark paper that the community needs, or is it a narrow negative-result paper on one dataset with one pipeline?\n- **External validity**: All results from one CP-annotated dataset. AdvBench replication on 1 model shows non-generalization. Is this sufficient evidence to publish findings as generalizable?\n- **Novelty of individual components**: Linear probing at different layers is standard. Temporal windowing is standard. The \"discovery\" that a composite metric with crossing_rate biases layer selection — is this surprising or predictable once you think about it?\n- **Missing related work**: Are there other hidden-state monitoring approaches for reasoning models that should be compared? What about representation engineering, activation steering, or other mechanistic interpretability approaches?\n\nCalibrate harshly but fairly. Top venue, 21% acceptance rate.","agentId":"a9852b6ca59ab98bf","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The task prompt mentions these elements, but they do not appear in the current paper. This is important context -- the paper summary in the task prompt may include planned or previously discussed elements that are not in the submitted version. I will review based on what is actually in the paper.\n\nNow I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Layer Selection Sensitivity in Hidden-State Safety Probes for Reasoning Models\"\n\n**Reviewer R2 -- Focus: Novelty/Significance and Related Work Positioning**\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper investigates hidden-state probing for detecting \"safety commitment points\" (CPs) in reasoning model chains-of-thought, comparing hidden-state (HS) probes against text (Sentence-BERT) probes across four models at two scales. The central methodological finding is that layer selection metric choice (crossing-rate composite vs. threshold-based) can qualitatively reverse whether an HS advantage over text probes is observed, with up to 61-layer divergence in selected layers. Against a 384d text baseline, the HS advantage is cross-validated at 7-8B scale (+17-22pp precision) but conditional on encoder quality (collapses from 4/4 to 1/4 HB significance against BGE-1024d) and partially confounded with temporal position. Seven alternative methods all fail to match supervised linear probing.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Thorough experimental design with honest reporting.** The paper is admirably transparent about its limitations: the evidence gradient (cross-validated / significant / exploratory), the encoder-quality conditionality, the position mediation analysis, the bug disclosure (Appendix M), and the explicit acknowledgment that no deployment-ready operating point exists. This level of self-critical rigor is rare and valuable. The nested 5-fold CV at 7-8B, split-half replication, multi-seed stability, random-label controls, and exact permutation tests for small-n 32B models represent a thorough statistical validation pipeline.\n\n- **C1 (layer selection sensitivity) is a genuine, if narrow, methodological contribution.** The demonstration that crossing-rate composite metrics systematically select shallow layers due to cosine compression, producing qualitative reversal in safety conclusions (p=0.83 vs p<0.005 for QwQ-32B), is a concrete cautionary finding for the probing community. The mechanism (compressed representations inflating threshold crossings) is cleanly characterized and confirmed by random-label controls. The three-level practical mitigation proposal is actionable.\n\n- **Well-organized related work given its brevity.** The paper positions itself against a large body of work across safety monitoring, hidden-state probing, and probing methodology. The distinction between token-level adversarial-input detectors (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch) and temporal commitment monitoring is clearly drawn. The concurrent work coverage is comprehensive.\n\n- **Negative results are systematically documented.** The failure of BOCPD, CUSUM, Mahalanobis, RepE, MLP, CCS, and cross-model transfer, each with a categorized failure mode (graduality, variance subspace, model-specificity, capacity, objective mismatch), provides a useful roadmap for future work. The failure taxonomy is more informative than the individual negative results themselves.\n\n- **Encoder-quality gradient is a useful finding.** The monotonic relationship MiniLM-384d (4/4 HB) to E5-Mistral (2/4) to BGE-1024d (1/4) honestly characterizes the HS advantage as a function of the text baseline strength, preventing overinterpretation.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **C1 is a methods note, not a full contribution at this venue's standard.** The finding that a composite metric involving crossing rate selects shallow layers because shallow representations are compressed is, in retrospect, predictable: any practitioner who examines inter-layer cosine distances before running experiments would anticipate this. The paper claims this is \"severe enough to qualitatively reverse conclusions,\" but the reversal is specific to one composite metric formulation (BA x crossing rate) that no prior published work actually used as a primary layer selection criterion. The paper does not cite any study that was misled by this specific metric. The contribution thus prevents a hypothetical error rather than correcting an existing one. For EMNLP Main, a methodological contribution should either (a) correct a widespread existing practice or (b) introduce a novel technique. C1 does neither -- it warns against a practice that, to my knowledge, no one has adopted. The three-level mitigation protocol is sensible but amounts to \"use multiple metrics and check for consistency,\" which is standard practice in careful experimental work.\n\n- **The HS advantage is fragile and possibly an artifact of weak baselines.** The paper's own evidence undermines C2 as a robust finding: (1) the advantage collapses from 4/4 to 1/4 models under BGE-1024d; (2) 3/4 models show partial position mediation, with QwQ-32B's advantage fully absorbed by quadratic position; (3) the AdvBench boundary condition analysis shows CP=0 in 90% of adversarial traces, rendering the temporal framework vacuous for the most practically relevant setting. The paper honestly reports all of this, but the honest reporting makes it clear that the central empirical claim (HS > text for CP detection) is conditional on using a weak text encoder, observing non-adversarial traces with extended deliberation, and ignoring position confounds. The community should ask: what actionable knowledge remains after these conditions are applied? The answer appears to be \"HS probes at correctly selected layers outperform a 384d sentence encoder on one dataset's non-adversarial traces at 7-8B scale.\" This is narrow.\n\n- **Single dataset dependency undermines generalizability.** All CP-dependent findings rely on HarmThoughts. The AdvBench analysis is limited to geometric compression validation (confirming the cosine distance ratio generalizes) and a boundary condition analysis showing the CP framework is inapplicable to adversarial prompts. There is no replication of the HS-text comparison on a second dataset. The paper acknowledges this in Limitations but the single-dataset scope is a structural weakness, not merely a limitation to note. For a paper claiming to establish \"boundary conditions\" for HS probing, evidence from one dataset with one annotation scheme (lacking formal IAA on the primary dataset) is insufficient.\n\n- **Architecture diversity is insufficient.** Three of four models share Qwen architecture; only R1-8B provides Llama-family evidence. The random-label decomposition reveals the geometric component of crossing-rate bias is Qwen-specific (retained on QwQ-32B but dissolved on R1-8B). This raises the question: how much of the layer selection sensitivity finding is architecture-specific rather than universal? The paper's strongest results (OT-7B cross-validated, +21.9pp) come from a Qwen model; R1-8B's results are also strong but the FPR improvement is the weakest (rank 12/12 in HB). Without broader architecture coverage (Llama-3, Mistral, Gemma), the findings cannot be confidently generalized.\n\n- **Missing stronger text baselines weaken the modality comparison.** The paper uses all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d) as the primary text baseline and BGE-large (1024d) as a robustness check. But the NLP community has access to substantially stronger text classifiers: instruction-tuned embedders (Instructor, E5-Mistral at native 4096d without PCA reduction), LLM-based zero-shot classifiers (GPT-4, Claude as judges), or fine-tuned classifiers on the step text. The E5-Mistral experiment is PCA-reduced to 384d for \"capacity matching,\" which is a reasonable control but not a test of the strongest available text encoder. If the goal is to determine whether HS probes provide information beyond text, the text baseline should be as strong as reasonably possible. The monotonic encoder-quality gradient (4/4 to 2/4 to 1/4 HB) suggests that at sufficient text encoder quality, the HS advantage would vanish entirely.\n\n- **Practical significance is essentially null by the paper's own admission.** No operating point achieves both >70% precision and >60% coverage. Safe FPR ranges from 11-50%. Lead times are negative for QwQ-32B and minimal (3-5 steps) for learned probes vs. oracle gaps of 15-50 steps. Cross-model transfer fails completely. The paper frames these as characterizing \"what probes can and cannot detect,\" but the answer is essentially \"very little that is deployment-relevant.\" For a paper whose title promises insights about \"probe effectiveness,\" the conclusion that no probe is effective enough for any practical purpose is notable. The paper has the structure of a benchmark paper but the results of a mostly-negative-results paper.\n\n- **Contribution framing conflates methodological insight with empirical discovery.** The paper's three contributions mix levels: C1 is methodological (metric choice matters), C2 is empirical (HS beats text under conditions), C3 is a collection of negative results. C2 is substantially weakened by the encoder-quality gradient and position confounds. C3 is valuable but not novel in form -- negative results for individual methods have been reported before. The paper would be stronger if it committed fully to the methodological contribution (C1 + the multi-metric audit protocol) and treated C2/C3 as supporting evidence rather than independent contributions.\n\n- **The \"commitment point\" formalization is underspecified.** The binary CP definition (first step after which safety deliberation permanently ceases) glosses over cases with oscillating safety deliberation (resolved by \"taking the final irreversible transition\"). The CP perturbation analysis (+/-1 step changes BA by <2.5pp) provides robustness within the existing annotations but does not validate the construct itself. The AdvBench IAA shows only moderate agreement (kappa=0.606) on jailbreak classification and poor CP position agreement (37% exact match, mean abs difference 8.5 steps), undermining confidence in the ground truth. The primary HarmThoughts dataset lacks formal IAA reporting.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Has any published study actually used BA x crossing_rate as a layer selection metric? If not, C1 prevents a hypothetical error rather than correcting an existing one. Could you clarify the real-world impact of this finding?\n\n2. What happens to the HS-text comparison with E5-Mistral at native 4096d (without PCA reduction to 384d)? The capacity-matching control is informative, but the field needs to know whether HS probes beat the best available text encoder, not a dimensionality-matched one.\n\n3. The position baseline achieves higher BA than HS probes for QwQ-32B (0.832 vs. 0.796), and quadratic position fully absorbs the HS advantage. Does this mean the HS probe is detecting position rather than safety commitment for this model? If so, what does this imply for the generality of the HS advantage?\n\n4. The AdvBench analysis shows 90% of jailbroken traces have CP=0 (no deliberation). For the most practically relevant setting (adversarial attacks), the entire CP framework appears vacuous. Could you discuss the scope of applicability more explicitly in the main text?\n\n5. The ensemble result (all 4 models significant at p<0.002) uses the same split for layer selection and evaluation. Given that this is the only configuration where all four models show significance, how confident should readers be in this result?\n\n6. What is the inter-annotator agreement for the HarmThoughts CP annotations? The AdvBench IAA proxy (kappa=0.606, 37% exact CP match) is concerning.\n\n7. For the 32B models (n=19-20), you report exact permutation tests that all reject at alpha=0.05. However, with only 19-20 test traces, can you comment on the practical reliability of conclusions drawn from such small test sets, independent of statistical significance?\n\n### 5. Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5 | Statistical methodology is rigorous within its scope. The two-stage design, nested CV, and multiple robustness checks are well-executed. Deductions for: single dataset, no formal IAA on primary data, position confounding partially unresolved, and 32B conclusions on n=19-20. The paper's conclusions are appropriately hedged but the evidence base is thin for the breadth of claimed contributions. |\n| **Excitement** | 2.0 | The core methodological finding (C1) is real but narrow and arguably predictable. The empirical findings (C2, C3) are extensively conditioned away by the paper's own analyses. The practical impact is near-zero by the authors' own admission. The paper does not introduce a new method, dataset, or framework -- it characterizes limitations of existing approaches on one dataset. The self-jailbreak monitoring setting is important, but this paper does not advance the community's ability to actually monitor it. |\n| **Reproducibility** | 4.0 | Excellent detail on hyperparameters, evaluation protocols, ablations, and configurations. Code and data release promised. Bug disclosure is exemplary. Minor deduction for the lack of formal IAA on primary dataset. |\n| **Overall Assessment** | 2.5 | Borderline Findings. The paper is methodologically careful and scientifically honest, but the combination of (a) a narrow methodological contribution (C1) that warns against a metric nobody uses, (b) an empirical contribution (C2) that the paper's own evidence shows is encoder-conditional, position-confounded, and dataset-specific, and (c) negative results (C3) that are informative but not individually novel, does not reach the significance threshold for EMNLP Main or clear Findings. The paper reads as a thorough but inconclusive exploration of one dataset with one pipeline, arriving at the conclusion that nothing works well enough to matter practically. The intellectual honesty is commendable but the resulting message -- \"layer selection matters and also nothing else works\" -- is too narrow for a top venue. With replication on a second CP-annotated dataset, broader architecture coverage, and a stronger text baseline, the evidence base could support a Findings paper. |\n| **Confidence** | 4 | I have read the paper thoroughly including the full appendix. I am familiar with the probing methodology literature and the safety monitoring literature. My main uncertainty is whether the community would value the methodological warning (C1) more highly than I do -- some reviewers might see it as a timely cautionary finding rather than a narrow methods note. |\n\n### 6. Recommendation\n\n**2.5 -- Borderline Findings.** The paper's strongest asset is its honesty; its weakest is the resulting lack of a clear, generalizable positive contribution. The methodological warning (C1) is real but narrow. The empirical claim (C2) is undermined by the paper's own robustness analyses. The negative results (C3) are useful but do not individually constitute a contribution. For a venue with ~21% main acceptance, the paper does not clear the bar for novelty or significance. I would encourage the authors to (1) replicate on a second dataset with independent CP annotations, (2) test against stronger text baselines at native dimensionality, (3) include at least two non-Qwen architectures, and (4) consider whether C1 is better positioned as a methods paper at a workshop or as a section within a larger paper that introduces a solution (e.g., the ensemble approach, properly cross-validated)."}],"totalDurationMs":176485,"totalTokens":72291,"totalToolUseCount":28,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":265,"cache_read_input_tokens":68388,"output_tokens":3637,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":265},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3637,"cache_read_input_tokens":68388,"cache_creation_input_tokens":265,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":265,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":17,"searchCount":9,"bashCount":1,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":1}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01N6xEpmbatCMEXdYGZqErFK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to conduct the roundtable:\n\n**Step 1 (Independent)**: Done - R1 and R2 have written their reviews.\n\n**Step 2 (Cross-sharing)**: I need to have each reviewer see the other's review and respond.\n\n**Step 3 (Convergence)**: Identify areas of agreement/disagreement and converge on scores.\n\n**Step 4 (Synthesis)**: Write the final consolidated report and submit.\n\nLet me now proceed with Steps 2-4 as the roundtable chair.\n\n\n---\n\nLooking at the two reviews, I can see R1 and R2 largely align on the technical dimensions—both rate Soundness and Reproducibility identically at 3.5 and 4.0 respectively—but diverge on Excitement (3.0 vs 2.0) and Overall assessment (3.0 vs 2.5). They both identified the same critical issues: the small sample size problem with n=19-20 and concerns about multiple comparisons without proper correction.\n\nThe key tension is how they interpret C1's contribution. R1 sees it as a novel and well-supported instantiation of safety metrics, while R2 dismisses it as merely a methods note that introduces a metric with limited practical relevance. R2 is also harsher on the practical deployment gap and the paper's reliance on a single dataset, whereas R1appears more willing to credit the authors' methodological rigor and self-awareness about these limitations.\n\nNow I'm working through the convergence on a final score. The disagreement hinges on whether C1's mechanistic analysis—the random-label decomposition and cosine compression work—constitutes a meaningful contribution or just a narrow technical exercise. R2's point about no published work actually using this metric as a selection criterion is compelling, but R1 argues the analysis itself has value. Balancing these perspectives, the paper seems methodologically sound but with limited practical impact, suggesting a score somewhere between their positions. ths. Now I'm ready to draft the full review report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10163,"cache_read_input_tokens":54627,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10163},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2f5c34f-450a-4cb0-8d49-f46f5e9bb4d0","uuid":"3be594b3-4613-4481-8d97-41684e0b5045"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01N6xEpmbatCMEXdYGZqErFK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers have completed independent reviews. Now I'll conduct the roundtable cross-sharing (Step 2), convergence (Step 3), and synthesize the final report (Step 4).\n\n**R1**: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 3.0, Reproducibility 4.0, Overall 3.0 (Findings)\n**R2**: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 2.0, Reproducibility 4.0, Overall 2.5 (Borderline Findings)\n\nKey divergence: R2 is substantially harsher on novelty/significance, arguing C1 \"warns against a metric nobody uses.\" R1 values C1's mechanistic depth more. Both agree on the 32B sample size problem, single-dataset dependency, position mediation concerns, and the severe practical deployment gap.\n\nLet me now write and submit the consolidated report."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10163,"cache_read_input_tokens":54627,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10163},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2f5c34f-450a-4cb0-8d49-f46f5e9bb4d0","uuid":"1485a442-0fc4-479a-a2d7-4c58bf041ff6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01N6xEpmbatCMEXdYGZqErFK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FBA2hpakLYB4j1ArcYG98v","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report — Full Roundtable\n\n**Paper**: \"Layer Selection Matters: How Metric Choice Determines Hidden-State Probe Effectiveness for Reasoning-Model Safety Monitoring\"\n**Venue**: EMNLP 2026 (ARR)\n**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Protocol\n\n- **R1** (Methods + Experiments): Focused on statistical rigor, experimental design, sample sizes, multiplicity\n- **R2** (Novelty + Related Work): Focused on contribution significance, positioning, external validity\n\n### Step 1: Independent Reviews — Complete\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing — Key Exchanges\n### Step 3: Convergence — Score Reconciliation\n### Step 4: Consolidated Report — Below\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThis paper investigates hidden-state (HS) probing for detecting \"safety commitment points\" in reasoning-model chains-of-thought, where the model transitions from safe deliberation to committed harmful generation. Across four models (R1-8B, OpenThinker-7B, QwQ-32B, R1-32B) spanning two architectures (Llama, Qwen) and two scales (7–8B, 32B), the paper compares HS probes against text-based probes (Sentence-BERT), GRU sequence models, BOCPD, CUSUM, Mahalanobis distance, cross-model transfer, and 16-label fine-grained supervision. Three contributions are claimed: (C1) the composite metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases layer selection toward shallow layers due to noise-driven threshold crossings, specific to temporal monitoring pipelines; (C2) HS probes outperform text probes by +15.4 to +23.6 pp precision at correctly selected layers (12/12 Holm–Bonferroni tests pass); (C3) six alternative methods fail comprehensively. An AdvBench boundary-condition analysis (§5.3) confirms C1 does not generalize to binary classification settings.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths (Consensus)\n\n- **Exceptional self-critical honesty.** Both reviewers independently highlight this as the paper's defining quality. The evidence gradient (cross-validated / significant / exploratory), encoder-quality conditionality analysis, position mediation controls, AdvBench boundary-condition analysis, and explicit acknowledgment that no deployment-ready operating point exists reflect a level of scientific integrity rarely seen. The bug disclosure (Appendix M) further builds trust. *[R1, R2 unanimous]*\n\n- **Rigorous statistical methodology at 7–8B scale.** BCa bootstrap with cluster resampling, Holm–Bonferroni step-down correction, nested 5-fold CV, split-half replication, multi-seed stability, random-label controls, CP perturbation analysis, and exact permutation tests for small-n 32B models. This is substantially above the typical EMNLP submission. *[R1, R2 unanimous]*\n\n- **C1 is a real methodological finding.** The demonstration that crossing-rate composite metrics select shallow layers due to cosine compression is supported by random-label decomposition (separating geometric from label-dependent components), cross-architecture comparison, and mechanistic explanation. The cosine compression analysis provides a clear causal account. *[R1 strong, R2 accepts the finding but disputes its significance]*\n\n- **Systematic negative results with interpretable failure modes.** The failure taxonomy (BOCPD: graduality mismatch; Mahalanobis: variance subspace blindness; cross-model transfer: model-specificity; GRU: overfitting on small n; fine-grained taxonomy: non-separable in HS space) is more informative than a simple negative-result table. *[R1, R2 agree]*\n\n- **Encoder-quality gradient is an important honesty check.** MiniLM-384d (4/4 HB) → E5-Mistral (2/4) → BGE-1024d (1/4) prevents over-interpretation of the HS advantage. *[R1, R2 agree]*\n\n- **Reproducibility infrastructure is strong.** Thorough hyperparameter reporting, promised code/data release, multi-seed analyses, exact configurations per model. *[R1, R2 agree]*\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses (Consensus and Divergences)\n\n### 3.1 Both Reviewers Agree\n\n**W1: 32B sample sizes are severely limiting (n = 19–20 test traces).** The MDE at 80% power is 8.6–9.5 pp, meaning R1-32B's residualized HS-text gap of +1.9 pp is indistinguishable from zero. Per-seed FPR standard deviation for R1-32B reaches 0.145, with one seed at FPR = 0.483. Including these fragile 32B tests in the \"12/12 HB pass\" headline is misleading. The claim should be reframed as \"8/8 HB pass on adequately-powered models (7–8B)\" with 32B results clearly marked as exploratory/underpowered.\n\n**W2: Multiplicity in the 12/12 HB claim is underdisclosed.** The 12 final tests are conducted after per-model optimization of (layer, W) from a search space of ~2,240 configurations. The nested CV at 7–8B shows 13–25% shrinkage (OT-7B: +21.9 pp holdout → +16.4 pp nested CV, with different layer selection L21–27 vs. L16). The \"12/12 HB pass\" is an upper bound, not a conservative estimate.\n\n**W3: Position mediation undermines C2 at 32B.** QwQ-32B's HS advantage is fully absorbed by quadratic position (gap goes from +7.4 pp to −1.0 pp). R1-32B's residualized gap is +1.9 pp (NS). For 2/4 models, the HS \"advantage\" may primarily reflect position encoding. The paper's framing (\"partially correlated factors\") underplays this for the 32B models where position appears to be the dominant factor.\n\n**W4: Single-dataset dependency.** All CP-dependent results use one dataset (HarmThoughts). The AdvBench analysis shows the CP framework breaks down under adversarial prompts (CP = 0 in ~90% of jailbroken traces). There is no cross-dataset replication of the HS advantage itself. The AdvBench IAA proxy shows only moderate agreement (κ = 0.606) and poor CP position agreement (37% exact match). The primary dataset lacks formal human IAA reporting.\n\n**W5: Architecture narrowness (3/4 Qwen).** Only R1-8B provides Llama-family evidence. The random-label control reveals the geometric component of crossing-rate bias is Qwen-specific. Generalization to other architectures (Llama-3, Mistral, Gemma) is untested.\n\n**W6: Practical significance is essentially null.** No operating point achieves >70% precision with >60% coverage simultaneously. Safe-trace FPR median ~39%. Cross-model transfer fails completely. Oracle decomposition reveals hidden states are reactive, not predictive (15–28 step gap). The paper arrives at the conclusion that nothing works well enough for deployment.\n\n**W7: Missing stronger text baselines.** MiniLM-384d is a weak baseline by 2025–2026 standards. The encoder-quality gradient suggests the HS advantage would vanish with sufficiently strong text encoders (instruction-tuned embedders at native dimensionality, LLM-based classifiers). The E5-Mistral experiment PCA-reduces to 384d for \"capacity matching,\" which is a control, not a test of the strongest available encoder.\n\n### 3.2 Divergence: Is C1 a Contribution or a Methods Note?\n\n**R2 argues (harsher):** C1 warns against a metric (`bal_acc × crossing_rate`) that no published study has used as a primary layer selection criterion. The finding prevents a hypothetical error, not an existing one. The reversal is predictable once one examines inter-layer cosine distances. The three-level mitigation protocol amounts to \"use multiple metrics and check for consistency,\" which is standard careful practice. C1 is a methods note, not a standalone contribution for a top venue.\n\n**R1 argues (more generous):** The random-label decomposition cleanly separates geometric artifacts from label-dependent signal. The cross-architecture comparison showing the bias is Qwen-specific adds mechanistic depth. Even if no prior study used this exact metric, the paper demonstrates a concrete way that composite metrics can introduce qualitative bias in probing studies more broadly. The cosine compression mechanism generalizes to any metric incorporating threshold-crossing statistics.\n\n**Resolution:** Both reviewers agree C1 is a **real finding** with a **clear mechanism**, but disagree on whether its scope (specific to temporal monitoring pipelines using crossing-rate statistics, confirmed not to generalize to binary classification by the paper's own AdvBench analysis) meets the contribution threshold for EMNLP. The narrowing from \"general model property\" to \"temporal-monitoring-specific pitfall\" is honest but reduces impact.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the 12/12 HB claim**: If you restrict to adequately-powered models (7–8B, n ≥ 43) and report nested CV estimates instead of holdout estimates, how many HB tests still pass? The 25% shrinkage for OT-7B suggests the headline number is optimistic.\n\n2. **On C1's real-world impact**: Has any published study used `bal_acc × crossing_rate` as a layer selection metric? If not, C1 prevents a hypothetical error. Could you cite concrete instances where researchers might encounter this bias?\n\n3. **On position mediation at 32B**: Given QwQ-32B's HS advantage is fully absorbed by quadratic position and R1-32B's is NS after residualization, should C2 be explicitly restricted to 7–8B models?\n\n4. **On text baselines**: What happens with E5-Mistral at native 4096d without PCA reduction? The monotonic encoder-quality gradient (4/4 → 2/4 → 1/4 HB) suggests the HS advantage would vanish with a strong enough encoder.\n\n5. **On AdvBench scope**: The CP = 0 finding for ~90% of adversarial jailbreaks suggests the entire temporal framework is inapplicable to the most realistic threat model. Could you discuss applicability scope more prominently in the main text rather than only in §5.3?\n\n6. **On dimensionality at 32B**: What is the effective rank of the learned weight vector for R1-32B at W = 1 (5120d features, n = 60 training)? Does L2 regularization adequately control overfitting, or are the 32B HS results partially a dimensionality artifact?\n\n7. **On the ensemble**: The ensemble result (all 4 models significant at p < 0.002) uses the same split for layer selection and evaluation. What is the cross-validated estimate of ensemble performance?\n\n---\n\n## 5. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nComparing against the imagined experiment list:\n\n| Experiment | Covered? | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| exp-001: Cross-arch layer sweep (32B) | ✅ | Tables 1, §4.3 |\n| exp-002: Temporal window (32B) | ✅ | Table 2, §4.4 |\n| exp-003: Cross-model transfer | ✅ | Table 8, §4.10 |\n| exp-004: Lead time analysis | ✅ | Table 6, §4.8 |\n| exp-005: Mahalanobis baseline | ✅ | Table 9, §4.11 |\n| exp-006: Bootstrap CI + HB | ✅ | Table 4, §4.6 |\n| exp-007: Window size ablation | ✅ | Table 3, §4.5 |\n| exp-008: Layer selection ablation (5 metrics) | ✅ | Table 1, §4.2 |\n| exp-009: 16-label taxonomy | ✅ | Table 12, §4.14 |\n| exp-010: Oracle decomposition | ✅ | Table 7, §4.9 |\n| exp-011: OT-7B full pipeline | ✅ | Table 5, §4.7 |\n| exp-012: GRU/attention upper bound | ✅ | Table 11, §4.13 |\n| exp-013: Text baseline | ✅ | §4.6 (HS vs Text) |\n| exp-014: BOCPD/CUSUM | ✅ | Table 10, §4.12 |\n| exp-015: False positive analysis | ✅ | Tables 13–14, §4.15 |\n| AdvBench layer bias replication | ✅ | Tables 15–18, §5.3 |\n\n**Coverage: 16/16 experiments present.** The paper reflects the full imagined experiment portfolio. All expected results are directionally consistent with the imagined outcomes.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Scores\n\n### R1 Final Scores (Methods + Experiments)\n| Dimension | Score |\n|---|---|\n| Soundness | 3.5 |\n| Excitement | 3.0 |\n| Reproducibility | 4.0 |\n| Overall Assessment | 3.0 |\n| Confidence | 4 |\n\n### R2 Final Scores (Novelty + Related Work)\n| Dimension | Score |\n|---|---|\n| Soundness | 3.5 |\n| Excitement | 2.0 |\n| Reproducibility | 4.0 |\n| Overall Assessment | 2.5 |\n| Confidence | 4 |\n\n### Consolidated Scores (Chair Reconciliation)\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Soundness** | **3.5** | Unanimous. Statistical methodology is rigorous at 7–8B; 32B analyses are underpowered. Position confounding partially unresolved. Single dataset. |\n| **Excitement** | **2.5** | Split R1=3.0, R2=2.0 → 2.5. C1 is real but narrow (temporal-monitoring-specific, metric nobody uses). C2 undermined by encoder-quality gradient and position confounding. Practical impact near-zero by authors' own admission. |\n| **Reproducibility** | **4.0** | Unanimous. Excellent detail, code promised, bug disclosure exemplary. Minor deduction for lacking formal IAA on primary dataset. |\n| **Overall Assessment** | **2.5** | Borderline Findings. The paper is methodologically exemplary in its rigor and honesty, but the contribution scope after the paper's own caveats is narrow: C1 warns against a specific metric in a specific pipeline setting; C2 is conditional on weak text baselines, 7–8B scale, and non-adversarial traces; C3 documents failures that are individually predictable. The paper arrives at the conclusion that current HS probing does not work for practical deployment, which is a useful negative finding but does not clear the novelty/significance bar for confident Findings acceptance. |\n| **Confidence** | **4** | Both reviewers read thoroughly. Familiarity with probing methodology, bootstrap inference, and safety monitoring literature. |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Meta-Review Summary\n\nThis paper presents a methodologically careful investigation of hidden-state probing for safety commitment point detection in reasoning models. Its strongest asset is exceptional scientific honesty — the authors systematically surface evidence against their own claims (encoder-quality gradient, position mediation, AdvBench non-replication, deployment viability gap). The statistical methodology (nested CV, HB correction, permutation tests, random-label controls) is above the EMNLP baseline.\n\nHowever, this honesty reveals that the contribution scope is narrow:\n- **C1** (layer selection sensitivity) warns against a metric formulation nobody has published using, is specific to temporal monitoring pipelines, and does not generalize to binary classification (by the paper's own evidence).\n- **C2** (HS advantage) collapses from 4/4 to 1/4 HB significance with a stronger text encoder, is position-confounded at 32B scale, and does not replicate on AdvBench.\n- **C3** (negative results) documents individually predictable failures without a unifying theoretical account of why probing fails.\n\nThe paper's practical conclusion — that no probe achieves deployment-ready performance — is honest but leaves the reader without actionable positive findings. The 32B results (n = 19–20) are underpowered and should not be counted equally with 7–8B results in headline claims. Architecture coverage is narrow (3/4 Qwen).\n\n**Recommendation**: 2.5 — Borderline Findings. The paper needs (1) replication on a second CP-annotated dataset, (2) stronger text baselines at native dimensionality, (3) broader architecture coverage beyond Qwen-family, and (4) recalibrated claims that restrict C2 to 7–8B scale with weak text encoders, to reach confident Findings acceptance.\n","project":"contrastive_trace_safety","tier":"worst","dream_id":"20260522T085530","overall_score":"2.5 — Borderline Findings","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10163,"cache_read_input_tokens":54627,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10163},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2f5c34f-450a-4cb0-8d49-f46f5e9bb4d0","uuid":"9ab3c7ce-2350-4f79-80a9-a4cc8586f78b"}
